N2  - Innovative transmission technologies are transforming satellite security paradigms. This chapter explores integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), an advanced approach that combines radar-like sensing capabilities with high-speed data transmission. This enhances real-time threat detection and enables dynamic beam adjustment to counter emerging risks. Computer vision (CV)-aided communication uses advanced image analysis to accurately locate ground and airborne terminals, optimizing beam alignment to within a few meters and significantly reducing interception risks by minimizing signal leakage. Multi-satellite secure transmission systems use coordinated beamforming across constellations and dynamic frequency hopping protocols to create resilient networks that can quickly adapt to and counter jamming attempts. Security-oriented radio frequency (RF) techniques, including sophisticated waveform obfuscation algorithms and adaptive power control mechanisms, further strengthen defenses against eavesdropping and signal manipulation. The technical mechanisms of each technology are examined in detail, including ISAC's joint signal processing frameworks that optimize sensing-communication resource allocation and CV's deep learning-based target recognition models that enable robust terminal localization in complex environments. This demonstrates their potential to redefine the landscape of secure space communication.
